# The Black Coach, an original orchestral scherzo in G minor by Arena Agent & SZ.
# Form: timpani-and-pizzicato intro - scherzo A (horns) - scherzo A (violins) - development in
# fragments - trio in B-flat (flute) - a quiet build on the ostinato - the scherzo in tutti - a coda
# that accelerates. 3/4 at 144 BPM, 168 bars, about three and a half minutes.
#
# The ostinato is the whole piece: the pizzicato plays six eighths a bar (root, root, fifth, root,
# third, fifth) with the downbeat accented and no two beats at the same weight, and the timpani
# answers it on beats 1 and 3, rolling into every section change. The horns state theme A over that
# grid, the violins take it an octave up, and the development breaks it into three-note fragments
# handed from the violins to the horns to the flute to the cellos, with whole bars of silence
# between them. The trio moves to B-flat: the pizzicato stops dead, the flute sings over an open
# string pad, a slow cello and rolled harp chords, and the timpani drops to a whisper. Then the
# ostinato returns quietly and climbs back into G minor for the tutti reprise and the accelerando.
#
# Borrowed from the bank, both through this style: the pizzicato strings ('pizzicato') for the
# ostinato, and the glockenspiel for the holes in the hocket and the last bar. No oboe, no bells.
#
# Built by its own generator, tools/arranger/the-black-coach.mjs, from tools/arranger/lib.mjs:
# the harmony, the theme, the figures, the voicings, the velocities and the microtiming all live there.
#
# Human feel: the first note of a horn or flute bar always leans in +10 to +16 ticks (never early,
# so it can never sound under the bar before it) and the inner notes sway -11 to +16; the pizzicato
# stays on the grid but its six eighths never land at the same velocity twice; the timpani rolls
# into every section change; nothing swings.
#
# chords: Gm Gm Eb Eb Cm Cm D7 D7
# chords @9: Gm Gm Eb Eb Cm Cmadd9 D7 D7 Gm Gm Cm Cm Ab Eb D7 Gm
# chords @25: Gm Gm Eb Eb Cm Cmadd9 D7 D7 Gm Gm Cm Cm Ab Eb D7 Gm
# chords @41: Gm Gm Eb Eb Cm Cm D7 D7
# chords @49: Cm Cm Gm Gm Ab Ab D7 D7
# chords @57: Eb Eb F F D7 D7 Gm Gm
# chords @65: Gm Gm Cm Cm F F F7 F7
# chords @73: Bb F6 Gm Eb Bb F Eb F7 Bb F Gm Eb Cm Eb F7 Bb
# chords @89: Dm Dm Gm Gm Eb Eb Cm F7 Bb F6 Gm Eb Cm Eb F7 Bb
# chords @105: Gm Gm Eb Eb Cm Cm D7 D7 Gm Gm Cm Cm Eb Eb D7 D7
# chords @121: Gm Gm Eb Eb Cm Cmadd9 D7 D7 Gm Gm Cm Cm Ab Eb D7 Gm
# chords @137: Eb Eb F F D7 D7 Eb Eb
# chords @145: Cm Cm Eb Eb F F D7 D7
# chords @153: Gm Gm D7 D7 Cm Cm Eb Eb F F D7 D7 Gm Gm D7 Gm
#
# Form:
# 1-8: Intro: timpani and pizzicato, a roll into the theme
# 9-24: Scherzo A1: the horns state the theme, the violins answer in its gaps
# 25-40: Scherzo A2: the violins take the theme over, an octave up, the horns calling
# 41-48: Development: the theme in three-note fragments, violins answering the horns
# 49-56: Development: stretto, two bars each for violins, horns, flute and cellos
# 57-64: Development: silence and sudden accents, the glockenspiel in the holes
# 65-72: Development: a build on the ostinato, the horns holding F into the trio
# 73-88: Trio: the flute sings in B-flat over sustained strings and soft harp
# 89-104: Trio: the middle darkens, the violins join on long notes
# 105-120: Build: quiet on the ostinato, a bar at a time, back into G minor
# 121-136: Scherzo reprise: the violins take the theme, the horns stabbing under them
# 137-144: Scherzo reprise: a rising sequence, the whole orchestra in fragments
# 145-152: Scherzo reprise: the build into the coda
# 153-168: Coda: accelerando, the fragments closing on one last hit
#
title: The Black Coach
composer: Arena Agent & SZ
year: 2026
source: original
category: originals
style: orchestral
key: G minor to B-flat major
tempo: 144
time: 3/4
about: An orchestral scherzo, a night chase in G minor at 144 BPM in 3/4: a pizzicato ostinato and a timpani pulse under a horn theme, the violins taking it an octave up, a development that hacks the theme into fragments across the orchestra, a lyrical trio in B-flat for solo flute over sustained strings and harp, and a coda that accelerates into one last hit.

@bars 153-168 tempo=148..186

